BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Cracks & Reforms: The 1920s Through the GW Collection — Washingt
 on\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261017T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261017T170000
DTSTAMP:20261017T203942Z
UID:1c592a103469ff68fc6d534b3e63@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:The 1920s emerged as a decade suspended between collapse and r
 econstruction\, an era marked by “cracks and reforms” that ushered in 
 a profound reconfiguration of the visual world.  During this period marke
 d by civil transformation and political upheaval\, artists\, journalists\,
  and makers had opportunities to reconstruct both individual and collectiv
 e identities. A century later\, Cracks & Reforms presents a distinct cross
 -section of this period\, revealing global currents of migration\, moderni
 zation\, and cultural redefinition through the lens of the GW Collection. 
 Students led by Associate Professor Bibiana Obler selected works to study 
 and exhibit\, their research eventually leading to unified t\nCategory: Ar
 ts & Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/cracks-refo
 rms-the-1920s-through-the-gw-collection
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/cracks-reforms-the-1920s-through-the-gw
 -collection
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Unfossilizing African Art — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261017T130000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-04:00":20261017T170000
DTSTAMP:20261017T203942Z
UID:5f9d2af2f85d5a2a49b31a14882b@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Arts & Culture\,Washington\, DC\,United States
DESCRIPTION:Presented after extensive research\, this exhibition recontext
 ualizes African material culture objects held in the GW Collection for nea
 rly 50 years. Led by Assistant Professor Clement Akpang\, 59 students from
  four seminar courses offered between Fall 2024 and Spring 2026\, conducte
 d original research to deepen understanding of the university’s holdings
  and update how we interpret and exhibit these objects. The exhibition fea
 tures 37 tangible heritage objects\, ranging from an Egúngún Paaka from 
 Yorubaland to a Kii [brass pipe]\, attributed to the Baumum people of Came
 roon\, accompanied by images and video that illuminate their rich historie
 s. Research on the entire collection is presented in an \nCategory: Arts &
  Culture\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/unfossilizing-a
 frican-art
LOCATION:Flagg Building\, 500 17th Street NW\, Washington DC 20006
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/unfossilizing-african-art
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:gwu-arts
END:VEVENT
